3. Build the new circular joystick scheme

For the Joystick scheme, replace the old lower-left Left / C / Right cluster with one circular mobile joystick.

Place the joystick in the lower-left area currently occupied by the movement controls and respect safe-area insets. It should visually match ALVA’s translucent circular touch-control style rather than looking like an unrelated third-party joystick.

The joystick needs a circular outer base and a movable inner thumb/knob. Use Pointer Events and pointer capture, consistent with the existing control system. Do not introduce an external joystick library.

The joystick is for horizontal movement. Dragging sufficiently left holds the existing internal left action. Dragging sufficiently right holds the existing internal right action. Returning to the deadzone releases both. Crossing from one side through the center to the other must release the previous direction and hold the new one cleanly. Releasing/cancelling/losing pointer capture returns the stick to center and clears movement.

Use a sensible deadzone so small thumb movement near the center does not make the fighter drift. Clamp the visible knob to the joystick radius. Vertical thumb displacement can affect the visual knob position, but it must not become Jump or Charge. Jump and Charge remain dedicated buttons.

Do not change the actual run acceleration or top speed based on analog distance. ALVA’s gameplay input is currently digital; the joystick should translate naturally into the existing Left/Right held actions.

The joystick must coexist with multi-touch. A player must be able to hold the joystick right while simultaneously pressing Jump, Punch, Kick, Shield, Shuriken, Special, or Charge with another finger.

Maintain touch-action: none, page-zoom protection, and all existing pointer-cancel/visibility/pause cleanup behavior.

4. Add Left mouvement and Right mouvement dash buttons

In the new Joystick scheme only, add two small dedicated directional dash buttons around the top of the joystick.

The left button should sit slightly above the joystick at its top-left side.

The right button should sit slightly above the joystick at its top-right side.

Their player-facing/accessibility names must be exactly:

Left mouvement

Right mouvement

Preserve that spelling.

They can use the existing left/right arrow glyphs so they remain readable at mobile size, but their aria-label/accessible name must use the names above.

A single tap of Left mouvement must request one dash to the left.

A single tap of Right mouvement must request one dash to the right.

These buttons are NOT replacement movement holds. They are dedicated one-shot dash controls. Tapping one must not leave Left or Right held, and it must not require a double tap.

Do not reproduce dash physics in TouchControls. Do not directly manipulate fighter velocity from the UI. Do not fake the dash by rapidly toggling Left/Right twice.

Instead, introduce a clean one-shot dash input request through InputManager, for example a queueTouchDash(direction) API producing transient dashLeftPressed / dashRightPressed fields in the sampled gameplay input. The exact API name can differ, but it must be a transient edge rather than a persistent held action.

In Fighter.update(), feed that explicit dash request into the same existing tryDash(direction, input) path used after normal double-tap recognition. The dedicated dash request should be consumed once and should not also count as an ordinary Left/Right press edge. Clear any pending dashTap state when an explicit dash request is consumed so the dedicated button cannot accidentally combine with a previous normal direction tap.

For #0001, one valid tap therefore starts #0001’s existing movement Dash with all current gameplay rules intact. If a future fighter lacks valid Dash data/art, tryDash() should simply reject the request normally. Do not hardcode id === '0001' inside the touch UI or input manager.

The dedicated dash button must therefore still fail naturally when the fighter is airborne, attacking/unable to act, already dashing, charging, holding Charge, shielding, appropriately holding Defense, exhausted, missing dash artwork, etc. It must pay the normal Dash Energy cost and play the existing Dash animation. Never bypass these checks.

Keyboard and gamepad players must retain the current double-tap behavior unchanged.

